A||||20||JohnnyK||IMportant question about Drum Brakes..||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||10-10-2000||07:49 PM||||I'm trying to fix my friends drum brakes, and I'm running into a big stumper... You push the pedal, and the cylinder expands and goes back a bit, keep doing that, and the cylinder eventually goes out, the seals fill with fluid, and it explodes (it's a new cylinder just put on today. i thouht that was the problem, but I guess not). Has anyone had this problem? It just happened all of a sudden (she was driving, pressed on the pedal, no brakes). You only have to press the pedal about 3 times before the fluid explodes out. What do you guys think? Please help! ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-10-2000||08:02 PM||||Please get a brake book before someone dies.<P>I imagine you mean the slave pops when the drum is together. If thats so the self adjuster has failed to work. There's a wheel with teeth at the bottom. That wheel need to be turned to make the adjuster longer. <P>There are other parts that make the adjuster move automatically. Some portion of the adjuster set is bad.<P>I'm not even going to try to describe the whole drum setup.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||10-10-2000||08:03 PM|||| Think about it, what's going to stop the pistons from traveling the length of the cylinder? You must put the shoes and drums on . The same thing will happen with disk brakes if you don't have the caliper and pads on the rotor. Assemble the system, bleed and then see if every thing works.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||10-10-2000||08:04 PM||||See, it looked like the problem was just that the seals failed on the cylinder (slave?? Wheel cylinder..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> but that isn't the problem... No body has had this specific problem?||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||10-10-2000||08:05 PM||||So that will always happen if the drum isn't on? But it did this WITH the drum on, and thats when the problem started..||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-10-2000||08:12 PM||||There are only 2 things that will alow that with the drum on. The adjuster problem above or you lost a part. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnnyK||10-10-2000||08:14 PM||||But without the drum, that will always happen. But without the drum on, I would think that the pads would still come back, but they don't seem to?||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-10-2000||08:21 PM||||Don't pump it with the drum off. It's not ment to work that way. The springs are intended to react only to small motions they get with the drum on. You are probably binding them and the slave pistons.||||reg||1
Z||000008||JohnnyK||10-10-2000||08:22 PM||||The shoes look really thin. Could this have caused the piston to pop off in the first place?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick||10-10-2000||09:13 PM||||<BR><B>Johnny</B>, it's one thing for you to be "learning" as you try and fix your clutch, but for gawd's sakes don't be screwing around with someone's brakes if you don't know what you're doing. You're scaring us.||||reg||4
Z||000010||JohnnyK||10-10-2000||09:20 PM||||Oh pshaw.. I rebuilt this car from nothing..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I've fixed disc brakes tons of times, but drums only a few times (I know whats safe and whats not.. Geez...) As for the clutch, I'm not screwing around with it, I found the problem.. just too lazy to get around to doing it..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I hate these drums though. What a pain in the ass.||||reg||1
Z||000011||JohnnyK||10-10-2000||10:09 PM||||Well, I have to do this tomorrow afternoon, so this is my plan. I already have a new cylinder on there, but the shoes must only be a mm thick. Sooo, should I get new shoes, put them on, and then see how she goes? I guess if I put the drums on there and then pump the brake tons of times, and then inspect, if it looks good, she's good to go? ||||reg||1
Z||000012||ThatRickGuy||10-10-2000||10:37 PM||||Okay, I've been threw the drum break thing a few times. 1st off, I'd say swap wheel cylinders, that one may be fine, but I get paranoid when I see fluid coming out. next, get new break shoes, they should be over 1/4" thick. then, turn the star so the shoes are way out there, to the point were you can't get the drum on, then slowly bring it back in until you can get the drum on. once you have the drum on, bleed the wheel cylinder. depending on the star adjustment system, some of them will only tighten when you are in reverse, and basically should take care of themselves. when you have the break together, befor the drum is on, you should be able to see if the star is working, there should be a little flipper flapper that should be able to adjust it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||JohnnyK||10-11-2000||05:57 PM||||Hmm, you know, they do seem more effective with the drum on...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Well, that was easy (I remember my van being wayyyy harder, and pieces flying everywhere.. Thank god for Japanese cars). But how much friction should there be when you put the drum on? I did it so it's fairly hard to take the drum off.||||reg||1
Z||000014||ThatRickGuy||10-11-2000||06:38 PM||||well, if it's too tight you may feel the rear wheels drag and lock up easy, there's a little slot on the back of the backing plate where you can put a 'star adjuster' (bent screw driver works equally well) to tighten/losen them manually.||||reg||1
Z||000015||JohnnyK||10-11-2000||08:02 PM||||I know man.. I'm not stupid (well, judging by my last post, I just overlook the obvious..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I remember I had to do that on my van.. Now, getting the other bleeder screw off, THAT is going to be a toughie..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||10-11-2000||08:15 PM||||If one side's tight and the other's loose, you have half the brakes on the wrong sides.  There should be a front and back (slightly different sizes) shoe for each side.||||reg||1
Z||000017||JohnnyK||10-11-2000||08:47 PM||||Yeah, I know there should have been, but there wasn't... Well, out of 4, one was slightly smaller, but I think that was just a manufacturing error. But oh well..||||reg||1
Z||000018||JohnnyK||10-11-2000||10:36 PM||||Well, they seemed to work (Could only bleed that one brake (left rear) and even then I just gravity bled it for a second)... But I hope the cylinder doesn't do that again.... ||||reg||1
Z||000019||ThatRickGuy||10-11-2000||11:00 PM||||when bleeding, you should always start farthest and work your way closest. that means right rear, then left rear. I've seen breaks where both shoes were the same, not bad to double check tho.||||reg||1
Z||000020||JohnnyK||10-11-2000||11:32 PM||||I know, I'm just saying I think I got away with just gravity bleeding the one.. NOt in the mood to wrestle with 12 year old bleeder screws.. I'm sure you know what I'm talking about..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
